{"author":{"id":"f943aa1c5ee078b881a8e97535a4e2ccb401d01ca00f4128126befa9cca58a32"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792494327,"nonce":"cChvPWGFVO8RmEmumynxBKhWXn4=","title":"review: flag a diff that disagrees with its declared risk class","message":"Raised 2026-10-20, while discussing the risk scale on the Cycle view.\n\ngit-board's risk class (low, normal, structural) is set by a person at `ready`, before any code exists, because it decides whether a plan is needed. The class is never checked against the diff that comes later.\n\n**Want:** in the Review workspace (dev-cycle M4, \"bots first\"), a flag when the diff disagrees with its declared class. Example: declared low, but the diff touches migrations, auth, the pipeline, the data model or emission totals, which are step 2's structural list. The flag says why. It's a check, never an approver: review still needs \"a human who can explain the diff\", whatever the class.\n\n**Prior art:**\n- *Automating Low-Risk Code Review at Meta: RADAR, Risk Calibration, and Review Efficiency*, Adams, Mockus, Rigby, Nagappan et al., arXiv 2605.30208, May 2026. A continuous Diff Risk Score from the diff, with percentile thresholds (P5 for human diffs, P20/P50 for bots). A diff below the threshold lands with deferred or no human review.\n- codeant.ai, \"AI-based PR decision making\" (2026-10-19). Low, medium, high and critical, mapped to auto-approve, one reviewer, two reviewers and a security sign-off. Signals: size and complexity, security-sensitive code, test coverage, author history.\n\n**Open:**\n- the signals: the paths from step 2's list, configurable in `[cycle]`, or a score from M6's model;\n- where the flag shows: the Review workspace only, or also the spine's step 2 row.","files":null}]}